\section*{Modularity of elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 030; 2026-10-04), the asserted result is that all elliptic curves over imaginary quadratic fields are modular with the stated local-parameter compatibility at every place.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every imaginary quadratic number field $K$ and elliptic curve $E/K$, the manuscript claims an isobaric automorphic $\pi_E$ on $\operatorname{GL}_2(\mathbb A_K)$ of trivial central character and weight zero with, for every prime $\ell$ and $\iota:\overline{\mathbb Q}_{\ell}\simeq\mathbb C$,
\[
 r_{\iota}(\pi_E)\simeq H^1_{\rm et}(E_{\overline K},\overline{\mathbb Q}_{\ell}),\qquad
 L(E/K,s)=L(\pi_E,s-1/2).
\]
For every finite $v\nmid\ell$, the Frobenius-semisimplified Weil--Deligne representation is claimed to equal local Langlands for $\pi_{E,v}\otimes|\det|_v^{-1/2}$. The complex-place unitary parameter is $\operatorname{diag}(z/|z|,\bar z/|z|)$. When $E$ has no CM the asserted representation is cuspidal. For CM curves it is induced automorphically, with a sum of characters instead when $K$ already contains the CM field. All local factors, including bad-reduction factors, are included.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 030; 2026-10-04), cyclic covers have one degeneration whose Jacobian splits into copies of the target curve and another that makes monodromy explicit. A geometric switch of coefficient primes and three automorphic lifts propagate automorphy through the family before descent to the original field.

